crucible is a team-based competitive  shooter published by  amazon and it's totally free from the  two hours we spent with it it looks like  there's a pretty steep learning curve so  here's everything you should know about  the game and what to expect depending on  the hero shooter you're coming from  i'm on fire crucible leans more heavily  on thinking  planning and team tactics than raw  shooting skills players take on the role  of one of 10 hunters  each hunter has an upgrade tree that you  work through mid-match and different  branches of that tree can be set before  a match starts  matches play out slower and more  methodically than other hero shooters  due to the maps being more open and  players having to split their attention  between fighting each other over  objectives or farming the environment  for essence  essence is needed to advance the  hunter's upgrade tree ideally at the  same pace as the enemy team  aiming still important but some hunters  tend to fall strongly into roles and  playing outside of that role may put you  at a disadvantage  for example there were cases where i was  playing as a support player and couldn't  close distance fast enough to do damage  to some of the speedier characters  because  of the early learning curve it's good to  just pick a jack of all trades character  to start off with like summer  once you got your hunter settled you'll  be playing three modes harvester command  is an 8v8 conquest style match and a  great place to start for new players  especially those coming from more  twitchy shooters it's the most  straightforward place to learn the map  which animals to farm  and what different types of plants do  some of them make you invisible some of  them give you buffs  some of them want to eat you it's also a  great mode to test out hunters vs other  hunters  keep in mind whatever hunter you pick is  locked in for the entire match  but multiple people on your team can't  have the same hunter once you got the  hang of that then move on to heart of  the hives mode  played 4v4 this is where you can apply  the knowledge you've learned part of the  hives includes a lot of the base  mechanics of harvester command but also  adds timed events and a different win  state  players compete to grab objectives that  spawn around the map some of these do  things like give you a permanent damage  buff the towers that you capture from  harvester command are also here and they  give you passive essence if taken in  hell  at certain points in a match a hive  appears and the first team to kill it  and  also collect its heart scores so if you  kill the hive but then  die the other team can grab the heart  and get the point three hearts  win the match in this mode you'll need  to communicate with your team because  team cohesion and timing is everything  these matches also sport random  modifiers that you'll need to understand  and take full advantage of  finally there's alpha hunters which  unfortunately we weren't able to play in  our session it's battle royale ish with  eight teams of two fighting to be the  final team standing the difference  is that solo players that have lost a  teammate can join up with other solo  players  but they can also screw them over at the  last minute which sounds kind of evil  some other things we noticed at the  event that you should know about are  there is controller support  although we'd only recommend it with  melee based characters there did appear  to be a battle pass  and definitely an in-game store which  wasn't particularly surprising  the game looks like it'll be a bit rough  for random solo players and it's best  played with a communicating team  overall crucible feels slower and more  nuanced than hero shooters you might be  used to  crucible is out on pc may 20th and can  be found on steam  thanks for watching and for more  crucible coverage head on over to  gamespot  and we're back  [Music]  you
 

